MultiPEM Docker workflow
========================

This directory provides one versioned Docker runtime for every registered
MultiPEM application and analysis. The base-R launcher creates an auditable
job snapshot and starts the calculation as a detached container by default,
so closing the terminal does not stop a long analysis.

Host requirements
-----------------

The host needs:

* R 4.0 or newer, with `Rscript` on `PATH`;
* a Docker CLI connected to a running local Docker engine configured for Linux
  containers;
* a processor architecture supported by the pinned base image, or Docker
  emulation for that architecture;
* permission for Docker to bind-mount the repository and results directories;
* approximately 1 GB for the runtime image, plus space for job snapshots and
  calculation output; and
* at least one logical CPU and enough memory for the selected calculation.

No host R packages, Bash, Unix utilities, Docker Compose, compiler toolchain,
or application-specific image definitions are required. Building the image or
refreshing dependencies requires network access. Runs require no network
access once the image is available locally.

`Runfiles-Docker` must remain beside `Runfiles`, `Applications`, `Code`, and
`Test`:

    MultiPEM_Toolbox/
        Applications/
        Code/
        Runfiles/
        Runfiles-Docker/
        Test/

On macOS and Windows, configure Docker Desktop to share the repository and
results locations with its Linux virtual machine. Native Windows users can run
the `Rscript mpem.R` commands from PowerShell or Command Prompt. A remote
Docker daemon is supported only when it can access every bind-mounted path at
exactly the path sent by the client.

Run the diagnostic before the first build or whenever the environment changes:

    Rscript mpem.R doctor

It checks the host R version, repository layout, Docker connection, Linux
container mode, and runtime-image availability, and reports the Docker server
version and architecture.

Run the host-side launcher contract checks after changing `mpem.R`, checkpoint
handling, or the application registry:

    Rscript test-launcher.R

The checks use disposable temporary jobs to verify clean rejection of unsafe
or unknown analyses, invalid stages and options, incompatible result
directories, missing or corrupted calibration checkpoints, and malformed
registries. They do not start a numerical calculation container.

Configuration
-------------

The launcher accepts these optional environment variables:

* `MPEM_IMAGE` selects the runtime image name and tag. Its default is
  `mpem/runtime:4.4.3`.
* `MPEM_RESULTS_ROOT` selects the parent directory for automatically named job
  directories. Its default is `Runfiles-Docker/results`.
* `MPEM_VOLUME_LABEL` may be set to `z` or `Z` when bind mounts require an
  SELinux relabeling option. Leave it unset on other hosts.

Set environment variables using the normal mechanism for the host operating
system or command environment. Command-line options such as `--results`,
`--cpus`, and `--memory` apply to a single run.

Build and run
-------------

From `Runfiles-Docker`:

    Rscript mpem.R build
    Rscript mpem.R list
    Rscript mpem.R run IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/I-SUGAR-hob

`list` prints every analysis containing `runMPEM.r` or `runMPEM_0.r` under a
registered application. `run` prints the container name and results directory.
Without `--results`, the launcher creates a unique directory under
`Runfiles-Docker/results` using the analysis name, UTC start time, and process
ID.

On Unix-like hosts, `./mpem` is an optional `/bin/sh` convenience wrapper for
`Rscript mpem.R`. The R command is the portable interface on every host.

Jobs run detached unless `--foreground` is supplied. Use the name printed by
`run` with the lifecycle commands:

    Rscript mpem.R jobs
    Rscript mpem.R status JOB
    Rscript mpem.R logs -f JOB
    Rscript mpem.R wait JOB
    Rscript mpem.R stop JOB
    Rscript mpem.R clean JOB

`logs -f` follows live container output. `wait` waits for completion and
returns the calculation exit code. `stop` terminates a running calculation; it
does not pause it or create a resumable container state. `clean` removes an
exited managed container while retaining every result file. Stop or wait for a
running container before cleaning it.

Containers remain available after completion so their Docker status and logs
can be inspected. An explicit `--name NAME` must be unique. Remove a completed
container or choose another name before reusing an explicit name.

Run modes
---------

The available stages are:

* `full`, the default, runs `runMPEM.r` and then `runMPEM_0.r` when the event
  deck exists;
* `calibration` runs only `runMPEM.r`; and
* `event` restores a pristine calibration workspace and runs only
  `runMPEM_0.r`.

Examples:

    Rscript mpem.R run APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS --stage full
    Rscript mpem.R run APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS --stage calibration
    Rscript mpem.R run APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS --stage event --workspace /path/to/calibration.RData

Use `--results DIR` to choose a job directory. A calibration and its event can
share one job:

    Rscript mpem.R run IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/I-SUGAR-hob-0 --stage calibration --results results/seismic-case
    Rscript mpem.R wait JOB
    Rscript mpem.R run IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/I-SUGAR-hob-0 --stage event --results results/seismic-case

When an event deck is present, a successful calibration must create `.RData`.
The runner copies it byte-for-byte to:

    RESULTS/.mpem/checkpoints/calibration.RData

The SHA-256 checksum is stored beside the checkpoint. Before every event, both
the host launcher and container verify that checksum and reject a missing or
changed checkpoint. The checkpoint is separate from the analysis directory's
working `.RData`. An event container mounts the checkpoint directory read-only
and copies the verified checkpoint to the working `.RData` before starting
`runMPEM_0.r`. Every event invocation therefore starts from the same
calibration state, even when an earlier event saved changes to its working
`.RData`.

Retain `RESULTS/.mpem/checkpoints/calibration.RData` and its checksum as the
pristine benchmark/calibration artifact for future event analyses. Do not
replace it with the event-modified `.RData` under `RESULTS/work`.

One calibration can seed any number of event cases. First retain a completed
calibration job:

    Rscript mpem.R run IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/I-SUGAR-hob-0 --stage calibration --results results/seismic-calibration
    Rscript mpem.R wait JOB

Create each event case under `../Runfiles`, give it a `runMPEM_0.r`, and create
a separate job from the pristine checkpoint:

    Rscript mpem.R run IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/New-Event-A-0 --stage event --workspace results/seismic-calibration/.mpem/checkpoints/calibration.RData --results results/new-event-a

    Rscript mpem.R run IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/New-Event-B-0 --stage event --workspace results/seismic-calibration/.mpem/checkpoints/calibration.RData --results results/new-event-b

`--workspace` is valid only with `--stage event` while creating a job. It copies
the supplied file into the new job's checkpoint directory and working analysis
directory. An event-only case does not need its own `runMPEM.r`.

Dependent MultiPEM optimization inputs
--------------------------------------

Some multi-phenomenology calibration decks use `opt.RData` files from earlier
single-phenomenology calculations as optimization starting values. These files
are different from the calibration `.RData` checkpoint used by `--workspace`.
The runner does not transfer them between jobs automatically.

Every job is created from a fresh snapshot of canonical `../Runfiles`. After a
source job succeeds, copy its optimization result from:

    RESULTS/work/APPLICATION/PHENOMENOLOGY/ANALYSIS/opt.RData

to the exact canonical `Runfiles/APPLICATION/MULTIPEM/Opt` filename required by
the destination deck. For example, after a rapid IYDT seismic job written to
`results/seismic`, stage its result for the rapid four-phenomenology deck with:

    mkdir -p ../Runfiles/IYDT-gsrp/4-Phen/Opt
    cp results/seismic/work/IYDT-gsrp/Seismic/I-SUGAR-hob-0/opt.RData \
      ../Runfiles/IYDT-gsrp/4-Phen/Opt/opt_1_0.RData

Stage every other required phenomenology in the same way, using the filenames
listed in the destination directory's README. On Windows, create the directory
and copy the files with File Explorer, `New-Item`/`Copy-Item`, or `mkdir`/`copy`.

Create the dependent Docker job only after all inputs have been staged. Job
creation captures the populated `Opt` directory; adding files to canonical
`Runfiles` later cannot change an existing snapshot. `--workspace` cannot be
used for this handoff because it accepts one calibration workspace only and is
valid solely with `--stage event`.

Job snapshots and output
------------------------

Each job contains a private snapshot:

    RESULTS/
        .mpem/
            analysis
            created
            image-id
            initial-files.sha256
            worker-controls.tsv
            checkpoints/
                calibration.RData
                calibration.RData.sha256
        work/
            Code/
            APPLICATION/
                Code/
                Data/
                PHENOMENOLOGY/
                    ANALYSIS/

The snapshot combines the selected application's canonical input decks,
application code and data, and application-independent code. The calculation
writes only into this snapshot and its checkpoint directory. Initial SHA-256
hashes record the exact prepared inputs, including CPU worker guards. Source
edits made after job creation cannot change a running job.

The principal console and status files under the selected analysis directory
are:

    multipem-job.out
    runMPEM.out
    runMPEM_0.out
    runMPEM.cpu-adjustments.log
    runMPEM_0.cpu-adjustments.log
    .mpem-runMPEM.status
    .mpem-runMPEM_0.status

`multipem-job.out` appends every stage invocation and is the durable combined
log. A deck's `.out` and CPU-adjustment log describe its most recent invocation
and are replaced when that deck runs again. Files for a deck that has not run
are absent. Docker retains the same console stream while its managed container
exists.

Normally, a status file records `running` followed by `complete` or `failed`,
timestamps, and the exit code. Immediate container termination can leave the
deck status file at `running`; use `Rscript mpem.R status JOB` for authoritative
Docker state in that case.

An existing results directory retains its snapshot and working output. The
launcher rejects reuse with a different analysis or runtime image. Use a new
results directory for an independent calculation or a clean calibration
rerun. Reuse a results directory deliberately only when continuing its staged
calibration/event workflow.

CPU and memory assignment
-------------------------

Every deck detects the usable CPU capacity visible inside its container. If a
deck requests more concurrent workers than are available, the effective worker
pool is reduced and the calculation continues. The warning is written to the
Docker stream, combined job log, deck log, and CPU-adjustment log. For example:

    MultiPEM CPU adjustment: available CPU count is 8; effective ncores_mc worker assignment reduced from 30 to 8; logical ncores_mc remains 30 to preserve calculation structure

The worker pools associated with `ncores_mle`, `ncores_map`, `ncores_mc`, and
`ncores_smc` are guarded. Their logical values remain unchanged because some
calculations use them to define chains, samples, seeds, or initial states. The
guard changes concurrency without changing the calculation structure.

Use `--cpus N` to impose a lower Docker CPU quota. The worker count is no
greater than the whole-number capacity represented by that quota and is always
at least one. Without `--cpus`, the container-visible host or Docker virtual
machine capacity is used.

Use `--memory SIZE` for a Docker memory ceiling, for example `--memory 16g`.
Memory requirements cannot be reduced safely through a generic input-deck
setting, so an insufficient limit can terminate the container.

Numerical reproducibility
-------------------------

The workflow preserves input-deck random seeds, algorithms, model settings,
logical task and chain counts, checkpoint order, and saved calculation objects.
Worker guards are added only to the private snapshot and do not modify
canonical files under `Code`, `Applications`, or `Runfiles`. The CPU profile
stores no helper objects in `.RData`.

The runtime also provides:

* R 4.4.3 in a base image pinned by immutable digest;
* exact package versions in `renv.lock`;
* versioned source and input snapshots;
* explicit calibration restoration for event inference; and
* an object-by-object `.RData` comparator with a default tolerance of `1e-12`.

Compare two calculation workspaces with:

    Rscript mpem.R compare /path/to/run-a/.RData /path/to/run-b/.RData
    Rscript mpem.R compare /path/to/run-a/.RData /path/to/run-b/.RData 1e-10

The optional third argument is the numerical tolerance.

Runtime verification
--------------------

The verification command runs global and IYDT application test suites in a
candidate runtime and an accepted runtime image, then compares their saved
objects at tolerance `1e-12`. It takes the canonical test runners from
`../Test` and the IYDT fixtures from `../Applications/Test/IYDT`; each image
supplies its own shared and application-specific calculation code. The
verification workspace is temporary and managed entirely by this workflow.

Create the accepted tag while the approved runtime image is selected by
`MPEM_IMAGE`:

    docker image tag mpem/runtime:4.4.3 mpem/accepted-baseline

When `MPEM_IMAGE` selects another candidate name, use that configured image as
the source of the accepted tag.

After making a controlled source, dependency, or base-image update, build the
candidate and run all suites:

    Rscript mpem.R build --no-cache
    Rscript mpem.R verify

The default accepted image is `mpem/accepted-baseline`. Supply another local
image name as the final argument when required:

    Rscript mpem.R verify my-registry/multipem:accepted

Run a subset with:

    Rscript mpem.R verify --global-only mpem/accepted-baseline
    Rscript mpem.R verify --apps-only mpem/accepted-baseline

These suites validate shared calculations, IYDT forward-model code, transform
and prior derivatives, and the production-data column contracts represented by
the Data suite. Before production use, also run representative input decks for
every affected model and compare their workspaces with `mpem.R compare`.
Stochastic comparisons must use identical seeds and logical calculation
settings.

Adding an application
---------------------

An application needs input decks, application code, and a data directory. Use
this layout, replacing the example names as appropriate:

    ../Runfiles/My-App/Phenomenology/Analysis/runMPEM.r
    ../Runfiles/My-App/Phenomenology/Analysis/runMPEM_0.r
    ../Applications/Code/My-App/
    ../Applications/Data/My-App/

`runMPEM_0.r` is optional. Keep paths used by a deck relative to its analysis
directory so they resolve inside the job snapshot.

Register the top-level Runfiles name by adding one tab-separated row to
`applications.tsv`. Its columns select the Runfiles, application-code, and
application-data directory names:

    runfiles    code           data
    My-App      Shared-Code    Shared-Data

Code and data names may be shared by multiple Runfiles applications. The
selected directories under `../Applications/Code` and `../Applications/Data`
must exist.

Application-wide code is copied to `/work/Runfiles/My-App/Code`, data to
`/work/Runfiles/My-App/Data`, and shared code to `/work/Runfiles/Code` inside
the container. When `Applications/Code/My-App/Phenomenology` exists, its
contents are also made available as `Code` under each immediate phenomenology
directory in the application snapshot.

Use the standard MultiPEM CPU names in new decks and application code:
`ncores_mle`, `ncores_map`, `ncores_mc`, and `ncores_smc`. Calculation functions
should use the corresponding local names `ncor`, `ncor_map`, `ncor_mc`, and
`ncor_smc`. Supply one of these variables directly as a `future` worker
argument on the same line:

    plan(parallel_plan, workers=ncor_mc)

Job preparation recognizes these worker arguments, adds the automatic guard,
and records every guarded location in `RESULTS/.mpem/worker-controls.tsv`.
Literal counts such as `workers=30` and unrelated variable names are not
guarded and must not be used for MultiPEM worker plans.

A source-only application addition needs no image rebuild when the required
packages and system libraries are already present. Confirm discovery and start
a new detached job:

    Rscript mpem.R list
    Rscript mpem.R run My-App/Phenomenology/Analysis

If the application needs another R package, add it to `runtime_packages` in
`docker/create-package-lock.R`, refresh the lock, and rebuild. Add required
operating-system libraries to `Dockerfile.runtime` before its package-restore
layer.

Maintenance
-----------

Package and base-image changes are controlled runtime updates. Before starting
an update, retain the accepted runtime tag described under Runtime
verification.

To change the R package set:

1. Edit `runtime_packages` in `docker/create-package-lock.R`.
2. Run `Rscript mpem.R refresh-lock`; this requires network access and writes
   `renv.lock`.
3. Run `Rscript mpem.R build --no-cache`.
4. Run the verification suites and representative application decks.
5. Accept the update only when saved calculations agree at the required
   tolerance.

When changing the base-image digest or system libraries in
`Dockerfile.runtime`, rebuild without cache and complete the same verification.
The Dockerfile places dependency restoration before source copies so ordinary
source rebuilds reuse the expensive dependency layer.

A runtime rebuild changes its image ID. Jobs record their image ID and cannot
be launched with a different runtime image; create a new results directory for
the rebuilt runtime.

Troubleshooting
---------------

Start with:

    Rscript mpem.R doctor

Common diagnostic paths are:

* If Docker is unavailable, start the local engine and confirm the selected
  Docker context can run Linux containers.
* If a bind mount is denied, enable repository and results-directory sharing in
  Docker Desktop or set the required SELinux volume label.
* If an event reports a missing checkpoint, use the completed calibration
  job's `.mpem/checkpoints/calibration.RData` with `--workspace` while creating
  a new event job.
* If a container name already exists, inspect or clean that managed container,
  or select another `--name`.
* If a detached calculation fails, inspect `Rscript mpem.R status JOB`, then
  the saved `multipem-job.out` and deck-specific output.
